One Pan Crispy Gnocchi
  --Jamie Vepsa

1/2 c chicken or vegetable broth
1/4 c apple cider vinegar
1 tbsp maple syrup
1 tbsp Dijon mustard
3 garlic cloves, minced
1 tbsp finely chopped fresh rosemary
1/2 tsp each kosher salt and black pepper
3 tbsp evoo, divided
16 oz pkg. DeLallo shelf-stable potato gnocchi
1 lb Brussels sprouts, trimmed and thinly sliced
1/2 c minced shallots
1 lb hot or sweet Italian turkey sausage, casings removed (sub pork sausage)
1/2 c (~1 oz) freshly grated Parmigiano-Reggiano cheese

In a large glass measuring cup or bowl, combine broth, apple cider vinegar, maple syrup, Dijon mustard, garlic, rosemary, salt, and pepper; whisk to combine. Set aside. Add 2 tbsp olive oil to a large skillet over medium-high. Break up any stuck-together gnocchi and add to the pan in an even layer. Cook 2-3 min, undisturbed, until the gnocchi are golden brown and easily release from the skillet. Stir, cover, and cook 2-4 more min, lifting the lid occasionally to stir, until the gnocchi are tender and crispy. Transfer to a bowl. Add sausage to the pan, still over medium-high heat, and break into small pieces. Cook, undisturbed, until browned, ~2-4 min. Stir and continue cooking until sausage is cooked through, ~2-4 more min. Add Brussels sprouts and shallots to pan, along with remaining 1 tbsp olive oil; stir to incorporate. Cover and cook, lifting the lid occasionally to stir, until the Brussels soften, ~5 min. Add Maple-Cider Sauce to pan, and scrape up any browned bits on the skillet. Simmer until the sauce reduces, ~3 min. Gradually stir in the Parmesan cheese, stirring until cheese has melted. Add crispy gnocchi and toss to coat. (The sauce will be light – just enough to coat all the components. Add an extra splash of broth, if desired, for a saucier consistency.) Taste and season with additional salt and/or black pepper as needed. Spoon into bowls and garnish with extra chopped fresh rosemary, Parmesan, and black pepper, as desired.


Notes: Slice the Brussels sprouts and shallots, and mix the maple-cider mixture up to 1 day ahead. Store separately in the fridge until ready to use.
